  "segments": [
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "When Artyom insults Gulya, Lyova knocks him down and a loud fight breaks out in the kitchen. Hearing the commotion, Eleonora and Chef Barinov rush in; Barinov seizes the chance to fire Artyom on the spot for fighting and for his disgusting cooking.",
      "gap": 0.8
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "А ну, хватит!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "lyova",
      "t": "А ну, извиняйся!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "eleonora",
      "t": "Что здесь происходит?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Всё нормально.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Поругались из-за девушки, молодые, горячие.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "eleonora",
      "t": "Из-за какой ещё девушки?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "lyova",
      "t": "Он приставал к девушке!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Ты уволен!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "За драку и за отвратительное приготовление блюд!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Лёва, ты молодец.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Не переживай, Артём.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Ещё найдёшь себе другую работу!",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Now let's go back through their conversation line by line and unpack the Russian phrasing.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "А ну, хватит!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Come on, enough!' An emphatic spoken line centered on the verb meaning 'enough'. Lyova answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "lyova",
      "t": "А ну, извиняйся!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Come on, apologize!' An emphatic spoken line centered on the verb meaning 'apologize'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "eleonora",
      "t": "Что здесь происходит?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'What is going on here?' In Russian, the idea of 'what' flows naturally into 'is happening' without extra filler words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Всё нормально.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Everything is fine.' Chef Barinov anchors the line on 'everything', while 'normally' completes the thought.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Поругались из-за девушки, молодые, горячие.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'They argued over a girl, young and hot-headed.' Chef Barinov brings out the contrast between 'to quarrel' and 'because of' in a few short words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "eleonora",
      "t": "Из-за какой ещё девушки?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Over what girl?' The grammar packs 'because of' and 'a girl' into a tight, idiomatic kitchen exchange.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "lyova",
      "t": "Он приставал к девушке!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'He was hitting on a girl!' Lyova brings out the contrast between 'he' and 'pestered' in a few short words. Chef Barinov answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Ты уволен!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'You are fired!' An emphatic spoken line centered on the verb meaning 'fired'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "За драку и за отвратительное приготовление блюд!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'For the fight and for the disgusting preparation of dishes!' Notice how Russian expresses 'for' and 'dishes' with crisp inflection rather than helper words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Лёва, ты молодец.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Lyova, well done.' Chef Barinov addresses the listener with the informal singular 'you' alongside 'well done'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Не переживай, Артём.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Do not worry, Artyom.' Chef Barinov brings out the contrast between 'not' and 'to worry' in a few short words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Ещё найдёшь себе другую работу!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'You will find yourself another job!' In Russian, the idea of 'else' flows naturally into 'work' without extra filler words. Alright, let's hear the entire conversation one more time from start to finish.",
      "gap": 0.9
